@@ replace
Run the adapter's Preflight check. If the API key is missing, give the `set-secret` command with the absolute plugin path and wait for the user, or offer the CSV export path. In Cowork, never offer `set-secret`: offer the connector or the CSV export path. Create the run folder `<run>`: `run_id` = `YYYYMMDD-HHMM-<source>`, under the imports folder from `python3 -I -B ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/lib/config.py paths` (`references/apply.md`).
@@ with
Run the adapter's Preflight check. If the connector is missing, offer the CSV export path (about 200 rows or fewer), or show the upgrade message (`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/reference/upgrade.md`, trigger 2). Size check: count the contacts before you create the run folder or copy the file (for a CSV, the data rows of the user's file where it is; for a connector, its total or its first pages). More than about 200: stop with the upgrade message (Hard rules, Size limit), and create no folder. Then create the run folder `<run>`: `run_id` = `YYYYMMDD-HHMM-<source>`, under the `imports` folder in the active config folder (`references/apply.md`).
@@ replace
Follow the adapter's Read procedure. Read every page of each object; if any page fails, the export is incomplete: say so and stop before writing. Flatten each object to one CSV in the run folder (`contacts.csv`, plus `deals.json` and `activities.json` when the source has them), with column headers that `csv_import.py suggest` recognises and a `Source ID` column. Detail: `references/apply.md`, Flatten.
@@ with
The size check of step 2 is done. Follow the adapter's Read procedure. If the export holds more than about 200 contacts after all, stop with the upgrade message before any write. Read every page of each object; if any page fails, the export is incomplete: say so and stop before writing. Save the rows in the run folder (`contacts.csv` for a CSV source; `rows.json`, plus `deals.json` and `activities.json` when the source has them, for a connector source), each with a Source ID. Detail: `references/apply.md`, Flatten.
@@ replace
1. `python3 -I -B ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/lib/csv_import.py suggest <run>/contacts.csv --out <run>/mapping.json`. Show columns, three sample rows and the suggested mapping.
@@ with
1. Propose a mapping of each column (or connector field) to a logical field: `name`, `first_name`, `last_name`, `email`, `linkedin`, `phone`, `location`, `title`, `designation`, `company`, `company_website`, `company_linkedin`, `employees`, `headquarters`, `status`, `lead_source`, `first_contacted`, `do_not_contact`, `notes`, `source_id`, `extra`, or none. Map a seniority column to `designation`; without one, the plan guesses it from `title`. Show columns, three sample rows and the proposed mapping.
@@ replace
1. Build `<run>/keys.json` with every existing contact (id, email, LinkedIn, Source ID) and company (id, name, domain), as described in `references/apply.md`, Existing keys. If you cannot read all of them, use the per-row lookup for imports of 200 rows or fewer. Otherwise stop and ask the user to add a Notion token (`/gtm:setup`), or to import a smaller file. In Cowork, ask the user to run this import from Claude Code with a token, pointed at the project folder, or to import a smaller file.
2. `python3 -I -B ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/lib/csv_import.py plan <run>/contacts.csv --mapping <run>/mapping.json --existing <run>/keys.json --out <run>/plan.json`. The script reads `owner.emails` and `owner.own_domains` from the config; pass `--owner-emails` and `--own-domains` (comma lists) to override. Rows with an owner address are skipped as `owner address`; add `--include-owner` only when the user explicitly asks. Use `--allow-keyless` only when the user asks and every row has a `Source ID`.
@@ with
1. Build `<run>/keys.json` with every existing contact (id, email, LinkedIn, Source ID) and company (id, name, domain), as described in `references/apply.md`, Existing keys. If you cannot read all of them, use the per-row lookup. If the CRM has about 300 contacts or more, or the reads stop on the query quota, stop before any write and show the upgrade message (`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/reference/upgrade.md`, trigger 1).
2. Plan every row yourself with the rules in `references/apply.md`, Plan by hand, and write `<run>/plan.json` with the Write tool. Rows with an owner address are skipped as `owner address`, unless the user explicitly asks to include them. Plan keyless rows (no email, no LinkedIn URL) only when the user asks and every row has a Source ID.
@@ replace
REST when `notion.access` is `rest` (always for more than about 200 rows if the token is there), one record at a time. Else MCP: up to 20 pages per `notion-create-pages` call, and verify each batch before the next one. After each record (in MCP, after each batch call returns), append its lines to `<run>/done.jsonl`, and update `state-set import` after each verified batch, so a stopped run resumes where it left off.
@@ with
Use MCP: up to 20 pages per `notion-create-pages` call, and verify each batch before the next one. After each batch call returns, append its lines to `<run>/done.jsonl`, and update `adapters.import` in `state.json` after each verified batch, so a stopped run resumes where it left off.
@@ replace
- Source blocked (connector missing or auth error, readonly_api exit 2): do not retry another route on your own. Offer the CSV export path for that tool and give the fix.
@@ with
- Source blocked (connector missing or auth error): do not retry another route on your own. Offer the CSV export path for that tool (about 200 rows or fewer), give the fix, and for a larger CRM show the upgrade message.
